Cave
You are in a large underground cave. The place is huge, though it does not seem like there are any exits, apart from a large hole in the ceiling, which goes up out of view (and must twist around, since no light comes from the top). Fear not, for light does come from several torches around the cave, and glitters from the giant mound of treasure you see before you. Gold, silver, jewels, diamonds, all sorts of treasure is here.  The most notable treasure is a large golden box with the insignia of a heart on it.

You can see a swirling tunnel of light and magic and a gold dragon here.

>x dragon
Ahhhhhhhhh! A gold dragon! (If you really want to get into this, scream whenever you read the words "gold dragon". I really mean it, in real life, scream! It's okay, no one will mind!) Anyway, the gold dragon (did you scream?) is easily the size of a few elephants. It eyes you curiously, though a glint in its eyes says it might just as soon eat you as say hello.

>x golden box
It is ornately carved with symbols of hearts on it. It almost sounds like you can hear a vague thumping from within.

>x treasure
Man, it almost looks like a dragon's hoard. There's enough money here, that you could be richer than Bill Gates and God combined, assuming that your sudden introduction of all these jewels into the economy didn't cause a rediculous inflation over the value of these metals. In fact, the fact that all this wealth is here presumable makes other similar wealth MORE valuable, by taking it out of circulation. However, the existance of dragons would probably work against such value, as you might fear that having gold would invite a dragon to come steal it from you. Economics of fantasy games rarely make much sense.

>x torches
They provide light to the room. And they seem not to burn out, now that you mention it.

>consult Wikipedia about dragon
Dragons are subtle and quick to anger. They collect treasure and will not share any of it. They are strong against damage and their very skin is poisonous (despite legends of dragon steak). Eating a dragon will eventually be deadly (unless it can be removed somehow). Dragons love snacks and will eat most food given, since they are immune to poison, this rarely goes badly for them.

>ask dragon about box
"Why should I answer you any questions? You're wearing winter clothes. You are obviously not a medieval warrior princess!"

