>consult Wikipedia about fairy tales
There are many popular fairy tales in the world. Amongst the most popular are: The Boy who Cried Wolf, The Three Little Pigs, Little Red Riding Hood, Little Miss Muffet, The Heartless Giant, Jack and Jill, Hansel and Gretel, and the Woman who Swallowed a Fly.

>consult Wikipedia about boy who cried wolf
Once upon a time there was a little boy who stood watch over some cows. One day he got bored, so to get a laugh, he started crying "Wolf! Wolf!" All the townsfolk came running, but there was no wolf. The boy laughed and laughed, and the townsfolk were pissed. 

The next day, he did it again. "Wolf! Wolf!" he cried. The townsfolk came again and got pissed when there was no wolf.

On the third day, a wolf actually came to eat his cows. "Wolf! Wolf!" he cried, but no one came, everyone thought he was just messing with them again. The boy watched in horror as one by one his cows were eaten by the hungry wolf. The end. The moral: Don't lie, or people won't believe you when you're telling the truth.

>consult Wikipedia about three little pigs
Once upon a time there were three pigs. They started building houses for themselves. Their father was a sorcerer, and he gave each of them one part of a spell scroll, in the hopes that they would one day work together. However, they each went out on their own to build houses, not really working together at all.

The first pig built his house of straw, the second of sticks, and the third, of bricks. They lived in their houses, not really speaking to each other, until one day, a big bad wolf came. He went to the house of straw.

"Little Pig, Little Pig, Let me in!" said the wolf.
"Not by the hair on my chinny chin chin!" said the pig.
"Then I'll huff and I'll puff and I'll blow your house in!" said the wolf. With that, he huffed, and puffed, and a huge gust of wind blew down the house of straw. The wolf ate the pig right up!

The wolf went up to the house of sticks. 

"Little Pig, Little Pig, Let me in!" said the wolf.
"Not by the hair on my chinny chin chin!" said the pig.
"Then I'll huff and I'll puff and I'll blow your house in!" said the wolf. With that, he huffed, and puffed, and a huge gust of wind blew down the house of sticks. The wolf ate the pig right up! He then went to the house of bricks.

"Little Pig, Little Pig, Let me in!" said the wolf.
"Not by the hair on my chinny chin chin!" said the pig.
"Then I'll huff and I'll puff and I'll blow your house in!" said the wolf. With that, he huffed, and puffed, but he could not blow down the house of bricks. The wolf saw the chimney, and he climbed down it. The third pig, caught unawares, was also eaten by the wolf. The moral: stick together, and use common sense.

>consult Wikipedia about Little Red Riding Hood
Once upon a time there was a young woman called Little Red Riding Hood. She went through the woods to go to her grandma's house, with a basket of goodies. On the way there, she was stopped by a wolf.

"Give me those goodies!" said the wolf.
"Like, no way!" said Little Red Riding Hood. "These are for my grandma!"

Seizing the opportunity, the wolf ran to grandma's house, gobbled up grandma, and disguised himself as her. When Little Red Riding Hood came over, the wolf was in grandma's bed, disguised as grandma. Little Red was fooled, though a little suspicious.

"Grandma, what big eyes you have!" said Little Red Riding Hood.
"The better to see you with!" said the wolf.
"Grandma, what big ears you have!" said Little Red Riding Hood.
"The better to hear you with!" said the wolf.
"Grandma, what a big nose you have!" said Little Red Riding Hood.
"The better to smell you with!" said the wolf.
"Grandma, what big teeth you have!" said Little Red Riding Hood.
"The better to eat you with!" said the wolf.

With that, the wolf jumped out of bed and attacked Little Red Riding Hood. She would have been dead, if not for a passing hunter, who heard the commotion and came in, shooting the wolf dead on the spot. As a reward, Little Red Riding Hood made out with the handsome hunter. The moral: Don't be too trusting of strangers!

>consult Wikipedia about Little Miss Muffet
Little Miss Muffet
sat on a tuffet
eating her curds and whey.
Along came a spider
who sat down beside her
and frightened Miss Muffet away!

>consult Wikipedia about giant
Giants are huge and consider themselves kings of the world. They often have palaces in the sky. Some go so far as to have no hearts. They are the worst kind. The only way to defeat them is to take their heart and break it.

>consult Wikipedia about Jack and Jill
Jack and Jill
went up the hill
to fetch a pail of water
Jack fell down, and lost his crown
and Jill came tumbing after.

... That's funny, you seem to remember it being "broke his crown." Perhaps it's a typo?

>consult Wikipedia about Hansel and Gretel
Hansel and Gretel were two children. Their parents couldn't afford them, so they led the children into the woods and abandoned them. Hansel was crafty, however, and he left a trail of breadcrumbs behind him to find his way back. He wasn't smart enough, however, and the birds had eaten all the crumbs before he could find his way home.

Hansel and Gretel wandered the woods until they came upon a house made of candy and gingerbread. They begain to eat the house when an old woman opened the door. "Hungry? Come in, dearies! I'll bake you a special treat!".

They went inside, but on the inside, it was all like a prison, and the woman was an evil witch. "And the special treat will be: you!" cried the witch. She captured the two children in the cell, and kept them there, fattening them up until she cooked them into meat pies. The moral: don't enter a stranger's house.

>consult Wikipedia about witch
Witches are horribly evil, though they usually possess magic wands. Magic wands are very usefull. Some witches are weak to water, some to bullets in the head, some to both.

