Lunch Spot
The forest opens up to a clearing here, where it looks like a beautiful picnic spot has been arranged under a particularly tall tree. The break in the trees gives a breathtaking view of the sky. Up in the clouds, it almost looks like you can see a castle, east of where the giant beanstalk is reaching. This particular area is very peaceful, the chirping of the birds and the song of the crickets almost seems to make a melody here, and the cool breeze is a nice touch.

You can see a tuffet and Little Miss Muffet here.

>x tuffet
Don't you know what a tuffet is? It's a kind of stool or chair, like the kind you might sit on.

>x miss
(Little Miss Muffet)
Little Miss Muffet is a small woman, technically not a dwarf, medically speaking, but short nontheless. She isn't adolescent or prepubescent or anything, just really short, about four foot three. She has flowing blonde hair all the way down her back (which isn't that far, actually). She is weaing a pink gingham peasant dress with a petticoat, red hair bows, white stockings, and a lace pink choker. She's probably in her late teens or early twenties.

>ask miss about tuffet
(Little Miss Muffet about that)
"I usually sit on the tuffet when I eat something. It's a nice spot here, don't you think?"

>ask miss about lunch
(Little Miss Muffet about that)
"Oh, I would love some curds and whey if you have any, but I'm pretty hungry, I'll eat whatever.

>ask miss about spider
(Little Miss Muffet about that)
"I am deathly afraid of spiders!" she says.

>give cheese to miss
(Little Miss Muffet)
"Ooh! Thank you!" she says, accepting the cottage cheese from you. She sits on the tuffet, ready to enjoy a light meal. Suddenly, a big spider drops down from the tree, right next to the tuffet.

"BOOGA BOOGA BOOGA!" yells the spider.

Little Miss Muffet screams, terrified of the spider. She throws the cottage cheese the the ground, and runs around madly for a few moments, lost in a panic of fear, before finally settling down.

"That was some funny shit!" says the spider, before going back up into the tree.

>take cheese
