>take wand
(the magic wand)
Taken.

>read arcane book
The book has several chapters: The Nature of Magic, The Source of Magic, the Types of Magic, and the Powers of the Wand.

>read nature of magic
 The nature of magic is very different than the nature of silence. Scientific laws exist as they do, and are the same throughout the universe. Magic works very differently. Some surmise that magic is not a law of nature, or tool, so much as being divinely ordained, a type of dues es machina. This explains why the nature of magic seems so random, arbitrary and nonsensical to the scientist.

 For example, gravity is the force that pulls all bodies towards each other, with a strength dependant on the mass of the body. However, the strength of magic is arbitrary, and different. One person can cast spells, while another might find themselves completely unable, even with the proper ingredients and spell instructions. That's why science can be called natural, as it is effective all throughout nature, and magic is generally referred to as personal, or specific to a person.

>read source of magic
 Magic phenomenon has been known to initiate itself to some degree when magical energies have been discharged in an area. Sometimes spells seem to appear in odd areas shortly after the discharge of magical energy in a place that has not seen magic in some time. This is known as magic overflow. For example, back in the Zork Universe, magic had been destroyed by the Guildmaster of the Magic Guild. However, some magic was preserved, and when time travel was used to obtain magical artifacts, it caused magic to reappear in strange and unusual ways throughout the empire. If you find that scrolls are appearing in places that they don't belong, it's a telltale sign that magic was used there recently, perhaps very powerful magic.

>read types of magic
Magic comes in three major forms: Spells, Artifacts, and Potions. Spells are simple enough, they can be used over and over once they've been properly recorded in a viable spellbook, provided that the spellbook is at hand and legible. Such spells can come from research or from scrolls. Since scrolls are generally the result of random magic overflow, the spells from scrolls are typically very specific and of limited use. Potions are created by special witches who understand the ways of alchemy. They can be drank, or thrown at someone to have an effect. Their effects are typically limited, wearing off after a period of time, though some of them can have permanent effects (like most transformation potions). Due to their limited use, and the need to carry them around, potions are not considered the best in terms of magic, though anyone can be affected by them, regardless of magic talent. Finally, there are artifacts, such as staffs, wands, or brooms. They are generally keyed into the magic of the user, and by pointing the item at a target and saying a magic word, a variety of effects can be produced. Many artifacts are specially keyed to help them be more powerful, if less versatile. For example, most wands are keyed to only respond to words beginning with the letter "F"…

>read powers of wand
Magic wands are generally keyed to special magic words, usually words which begin with the letter "F". To properly use a magic wand, you first point the wand at something. Then you CHANT "(the magic word)". Beyond the basic magic words like "Freeze" and "Fire", the word "Frobozzle" has long been known as a special word, which attempts to rip open a portal which has been closed by powerful magic. Of course, this would require somehow knowing where a portal was opened, and that portal must have been jammed open by a previous use of the Frobozzle word, and you would have to point the wand at that location, and cast Frobozzle yourself in order to re-open the portal. All that being said, it's not particularly likely to find a use for the Frobozzle spell.

